The derived file is app/src/main/assets/emoji.tsv, generated by tools/build_emoji.py.

The English word list in app/src/main/assets/en-frequency.txt
is derived from wordfreq 3.1.1 by Robyn Speer (Copyright 2022 Robyn Speer):
https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq and https://pypi.org/project/wordfreq/3.1.1/.
This filtered, frequency-ranked list is licensed CC BY-SA 4.0:
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/.
Upstream data credits: Google Books Ngrams (http://books.google.com/ngrams),
the Leeds Internet Corpus, Wikipedia, ParaCrawl, OPUS OpenSubtitles 2018
(https://www.opensubtitles.org/), and the freely available SUBTLEX word lists
by Marc Brysbaert et al. (http://crr.ugent.be/programs-data/subtitle-frequencies).
See https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq/blob/master/NOTICE.md for upstream notices.
TeleHex retains the first 50,000 unique ASCII words and contractions, with
curly apostrophes normalized, using tools/build_english_dictionary.py.

The optional sample English n-gram model described in docs/english-dictionary.md
is derived only from the Tatoeba English CC0 sentence export, downloaded 2026-11-08:
https://downloads.tatoeba.org/exports/per_language/eng/eng_sentences_CC0.tsv.bz2.
Source: Tatoeba contributors, https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads.
The source sentences and derived model are available under CC0 1.0:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/.
TeleHex does not bundle language models; users build and import them separately.
Build details and checksums: docs/english-dictionary.md.
English word-list attribution notices are also bundled in assets/en-NOTICE.txt.

The Vietnamese word list in app/src/main/assets/vi-frequency.txt is also derived
from wordfreq 3.1.1 by Robyn Speer, under CC BY-SA 4.0, with the same upstream
credits listed above. TeleHex normalizes Unicode, removes duplicates and retains
Latin/Vietnamese letter tokens up to 8 characters. See docs/vietnamese-dictionary.md
and assets/vi-NOTICE.txt for details. This word list is separate from vi.ngram.

